        "id": 0,
        "original_id": "ee05cd55-bbf8-539d-8499-1bf4605beb2e",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Edmund Bacon, Thomas Jefferson\n- Places: Monticello, Charlottesville, Virginia, Piedmont region\n- Roles: business manager, primary overseer, President of the United States\n- Topics: plantation agriculture, slavery, tobacco, wheat\n\nFacts:\n- Thomas Jefferson began designing and building Monticello at age 26 after inheriting the land from his father.\n- Monticello was the primary plantation of Thomas Jefferson, the third President of the United States, and it was located just outside Charlottesville, Virginia, in the Piedmont region.\n- Monticello originally comprised 5,000 acres.\n- Thomas Jefferson used enslaved people at Monticello for extensive cultivation of tobacco and mixed crops, later shifting from tobacco to wheat in response to changing markets.\n- Edmund Bacon (1785–1866) served for 20 years as Thomas Jefferson's business manager and primary overseer at Monticello.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
